@*
    PostTocRail.razor
    Purpose: Sticky in-page table-of-contents rail for the post detail page, built from the
    rendered article's h2/h3 headings.

    REQ-UI-045 / UAT-027 — post detail page rebuild (owner: "lot of gap on both sides" — the page
    around the 820px article column was empty; this rail is one of the two structures that now use
    that space). Wraps TrBlazeUI's <AnchorNav> for the list markup and scroll-spy "current section"
    highlighting (its own IntersectionObserver-driven JS, shipped as anchor-nav.js).

    Click handling is NOT left to AnchorNav's own <a href="#id">, though — measured live on this app:
    a bare "#id" href resolves against <base href="/">, not the current document path, so on any post
    page ("/post/{slug}") the browser (and Blazor's own click interception, which performs the same
    base-relative resolution to decide whether a click is a same-page fragment jump) computes the
    target as "/#id" and silently navigates the whole app to Home, discarding the article. post-toc-
    rail.js intercepts the click on this component's own root — an ANCESTOR of the links — in the
    bubble phase, which always runs ahead of Blazor's document-level listener regardless of
    attachment order, calls preventDefault() before Blazor ever sees the click, then scrolls and
    updates the address bar itself using the current pathname. See that file's header comment for the
    full write-up; filed as TR-074 in docs/TechieBlog-TrBlazeUI-Feedback.md.

    Deliberately renders NOTHING when fewer than 2 headings are supplied — a single-entry TOC is
    noise, not navigation. PostView.razor already gates on the same rule before instantiating this
    component; the check is repeated here so the component is safe to reuse on its own.

    Visibility below ~1024px is CSS-only — see `.post-toc-rail { display: none; }` in post.css,
    lifted at the >=1024px breakpoint — so the rail is genuinely absent from the layout on a laptop
    or phone (offsetParent === null), not merely squashed by a narrower grid track.

    AnchorNavSection carries only (Id, Label) — no heading-level parameter — so level-3 headings are
    given a non-breaking-space indent in their label text to keep the visual hierarchy without
    forking the library's own item rendering.

    Dependencies: TrBlazeUI.Components.AnchorNav (AnchorNav, AnchorNavSection, AnchorNavOrientation);
    wwwroot/js/post-toc-rail.js.
*@

@using TrBlazeUI.Components.AnchorNav
@implements IAsyncDisposable
@inject IJSRuntime JS

@if (Headings is { Count: >= 2 })
{
    <div class="post-toc-rail" data-testid="post-toc-rail" @ref="railElement">
        <TypographySmall Class="post-toc-rail__heading">On this page</TypographySmall>
        <AnchorNav Sections="@sections"
                   @bind-ActiveId="activeId"
                   TopOffset="96"
                   Orientation="AnchorNavOrientation.Vertical"
                   AriaLabel="Table of contents"
                   data-testid="post-toc-rail-nav" />
    </div>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Non-breaking space used to visually indent a level-3 heading's label, since
    /// <see cref="AnchorNavSection"/> carries no heading-level parameter of its own and a regular
    /// space would collapse in the rendered HTML.
    /// </summary>
    private const string SubHeadingIndent = "   ";

    private const string ModulePath = "/_content/BlogUI/js/post-toc-rail.js";

    /// <summary>
    /// The headings to list, in document order. Fewer than two renders nothing.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public List<TocHeading>? Headings { get; set; }

    private List<AnchorNavSection> sections = new();
    private string? activeId;
    private ElementReference railElement;
    private IJSObjectReference? module;

    /// <inheritdoc />
    protected override void OnParametersSet()
    {
        sections = (Headings ?? new List<TocHeading>())
            .Select(ToSection)
            .ToList();
    }

    /// <inheritdoc />
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para><b>Business Logic:</b> The click-interception script (post-toc-rail.js) is bound to the
    /// rail's own root element, so it only needs (re-)attaching once that element exists — which is
    /// every render where the rail itself is present, since the whole rail is conditionally omitted
    /// rather than hidden. The module's own <c>init</c> is idempotent (guards on a data attribute),
    /// so calling it again after a later render is harmless.</para>
    /// <para><b>Side Effects:</b> Imports and caches <see cref="module"/> on first use; attaches a
    /// click listener to <see cref="railElement"/> via JS interop.</para>
    /// </remarks>
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (Headings is not { Count: >= 2 })
        {
            return;
        }

        try
        {
            module ??= await JS.InvokeAsync<IJSObjectReference>("import", ModulePath);
            await module.InvokeVoidAsync("init", railElement);
        }
        catch (Exception ex) when (ex is JSException or InvalidOperationException or ObjectDisposedException or TaskCanceledException)
        {
            // Best effort — a reader can still use the anchors' native fragment scroll if this fails
            // to attach; losing the TOC's click fix is not worth failing the article render over.
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Builds one AnchorNav section from a heading.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para><b>Business Logic:</b> <see cref="AnchorNavSection"/> has no heading-level parameter of
    /// its own, so a level-3 heading is indented with <see cref="SubHeadingIndent"/> baked into its
    /// label text — the only lever this library API exposes for visual hierarchy without a custom
    /// item template.</para>
    /// <para><b>Side Effects:</b> None.</para>
    /// </remarks>
    /// <param name="heading">Source heading.</param>
    /// <returns>The section AnchorNav renders and scroll-spies against.</returns>
    private static AnchorNavSection ToSection(TocHeading heading)
    {
        var label = heading.Level >= 3 ? SubHeadingIndent + heading.Text : heading.Text;
        return new AnchorNavSection(heading.Id, label);
    }

    /// <inheritdoc />
    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        if (module is null)
        {
            return;
        }

        try
        {
            await module.DisposeAsync();
        }
        catch (JSDisconnectedException)
        {
            // Circuit already gone — nothing left to release.
        }
    }
}
